    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # One Step In, Two Steps Out! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Dance!</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> One Step In, Two Steps Out!</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can draw and label simple movements in pictures (e.g., drawing a person jumping and writing 'jump').
            Can engage in interactive labeling activities by matching labels to pictures of simple movements (e.g., 'run' for a picture of running).
            Can trace letters and simple movement-related words (e.g., 'hop' or 'run').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Follow the Leader Dance" using simple commands. The teacher says a command and acts it out, then students follow. Examples: "One step to the front!", "Jump up!", "Shake your foot!", "Spin around!". This activates prior knowledge of movement verbs and helps students physically connect to the vocabulary. Teacher emphasizes pronunciation of /t/ in 'two' and /d/ in 'dance' as they give commands like "Two steps back" or "Let's dance!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a large picture of a child performing an action (e.g., 'jump'). The teacher clearly says "This is 'jump'." and traces the word 'jump' on a whiteboard or large flashcard, emphasizing the letter 'J'. They then show a blank space next to the picture and say, "We will write 'jump' here." The teacher models tracing the word 'jump' slowly, articulating the sounds. They also show a picture of a 'line' and a 'circle', saying the words and tracing their shapes in the air, connecting to positional prepositions like 'jump over the line'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of someone jumping) "Is this 'step' or 'jump'?" (Students point to or say 'jump'.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing the word 'hop' on a card) "What letter is this?" (Pointing to 'H'). "What sound does 'H' make?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Dance!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large flashcards (A4 size) showing various dance movements (e.g., 'jump', 'hop', 'spin', 'step', 'shake', 'turn') with the corresponding word written clearly below each picture. Introduce new words like 'twirl'. Also, cards showing objects like 'chair' and 'line' for positional commands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Recognizing letters of the alphabet in familiar words (e.g., 'J' in 'jump') and introducing letters and sounds (S, T, U, V).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays a set of picture cards with movement actions and corresponding word labels (e.g., 'jump', 'hop', 'step', 'spin').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says a word (e.g., "Jump!"). Students point to the correct picture card and the word 'jump'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up a picture (e.g., a person stepping over a line) and asks, "What movement is this?" Then, "Where is the word 'step'?" Students identify the word by pointing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a card with the word "turn" and asks students to make the 'T' sound and act out 'turn'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Tracing letters and simple movement-related words; matching labels to pictures of simple movements. Using verbs (move, dance, step, spin, turn, twirl, shake, hop, jump) and positional prepositions (in, out, around, over).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Tracing Dance Moves:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures of children performing various dance movements (e.g., 'jump', 'hop', 'step to the side', 'spin around', 'jump over the line'). Below each picture, the corresponding movement word is written in large, dotted letters. Students use pencils or crayons to trace the dotted words, focusing on letter formation. The teacher circulates, providing individual help and reinforcing the sounds of letters (e.g., "Good job tracing 'J' in 'jump'!") and reminding them of the pronunciation of /t/ and /d/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Matching Labels to Pictures:</b> The teacher provides another worksheet with different dance movement pictures and a word bank (e.g., 'jump', 'step', 'spin', 'shake', 'turn') printed separately as small cut-out labels. Students cut out the word labels and glue or write them next to the correct picture. The teacher guides by demonstrating one example. For an extra challenge, some labels might include simple directive phrases like 'step up' or 'move around'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Label Check:</b> Students work in pairs. They exchange their completed matching worksheets. One student points to a picture and says the movement (e.g., "This is 'shake'."), and their partner checks if the word 'shake' is correctly matched or written. They use polite expressions like "Is this correct?" or "Yes, that's 'shake'!". Teacher encourages students to recognize letters like 'S' in 'shake' and 'step'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Dance!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "My Dance Move Story" - Draw and Attempt to Label.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a blank paper divided into 3 boxes or a simple template with three blank spaces. The teacher prompts: "Think of three different dance moves! First, draw one move. Next, draw another move. Then, draw a third move." Students draw simple stick figures performing movements like 'jump', 'hop', 'spin', 'step in', 'step out', 'shake fast', 'turn slow'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After drawing, students are encouraged to write the word for each movement below their drawing. They can refer to the classroom word wall or flashcards for help with spelling. For Kinder, it's acceptable if they only write the initial letter or a phonetic approximation, but the teacher encourages them to attempt the full word if possible, or trace a pre-written word below their drawing. For example, a student draws a person jumping and then attempts to write 'jump' or traces 'jump' if provided.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher circulates, offering support and asking questions like "What dance move is this? Can you write 'jump'?" They reinforce phonemic awareness by asking, "What letter does 'spin' start with?" (S).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and collection of student "My Dance Move Story" worksheets.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Dance Move Story" worksheet with drawings and attempted labels.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of chosen labels for pictures (if matching).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility and correct letter formation when tracing words like 'jump', 'step', 'spin'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort in drawing movements that match the intended action.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition and attempt to use letters from target words (e.g., writing 'J' for 'jump').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher shows two pictures: one of a child successfully tracing a word, and another of a child struggling. Students point to the picture that shows how they felt about tracing words today. Alternatively, they answer:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can trace 'jump'!" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can draw a dance move." (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word did you like to write or trace today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which dance move did you draw? Was it 'spin' or 'shake'?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this writing-focused lesson for Kinder students? The tracing activities were effective for fine motor skill development and letter recognition. Students enjoyed matching words to pictures and attempting to draw and label their own dance moves. Some students needed more individual support with letter formation, especially with less common letters or longer words. Scaffolds like pre-printed dotted words were crucial. The integration of phonemic awareness by highlighting 'J' in 'jump' and 'S' in 'step' was beneficial. For the next lesson, more guided practice on sequencing actions (First, Next) through drawing and simple labeling will be needed to transition towards narrative writing or dance instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration) will focus on consolidating knowledge by creating a simple "Our Dance Book" or "Dance Instruction Card," where students combine their labeled drawings from this lesson into a sequence (e.g., "First, jump. Next, spin.") and share it with peers, reinforcing both writing and speaking/listening skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time]</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>